587:
(a) The admission into the public sewers of any waters or wastes having:
(1) A five-day biochemical oxygen demand greater than 300 milligrams per liter;
(2) A chemical oxygen demand (COD) greater than 600 milligrams per liter;
(3) More than 350 milligrams per liter of suspended solids; or
(4) Any quantity of substances having the characteristics described in section 42-171;
shall be prohibited from discharge without preliminary treatment.
(b) The owner shall provide, at his expense, such preliminary treatment as may be necessary to:
(1) Reduce the biochemical oxygen demand to 300 milligrams per liter and the chemical oxygen demand to 600 milligrams per liter and the suspended solids to 350 milligrams per liter;
(2) Reduce objectionable characteristics or constituents to within the maximum limits provided for in section 42-171; or
(3) Control the quantities and rates of discharge of such waters or wastes. Plans, specifications and any other pertinent information relating to proposed preliminary treatment facilities shall be submitted for the approval of the superintendent, and no construction of such facilities shall be commenced until said approval is obtained in writing.
(Ord. of 10-8-1984, § 4-5)

620:
The following uses or conditions shall be and are hereby prohibited within wellhead protection zones, whether or not such use or condition may otherwise be ordinarily included as a part of a use permitted under section 42-344:
(1) Surface use or storage of hazardous material, expressly including commercial use of agricultural pesticides;
(2) Septic tanks or drain fields appurtenant thereto;
(3) Impervious surfaces other than roofs of buildings, and streets, driveways and walks serving buildings permitted under section 42-344;
(4) Sanitary landfills;
(5) Hazardous waste disposal sites;
(6) Stormwater infiltration basins;
(7) Underground storage tanks;
(8) Sanitary sewer lines within 150 feet of a wellhead.
(Ord. No. 12-09-97, § 5, 12-9-1997)
